It took me about 4-5 yrs of off-and-on recreational playing to finally
ascend. But honestly the Valkyrie I finally ascended didn't seem to
have particularly good fortune (tho not exceptionally bad fortune
either. I will admit I did use a few wishes and genocides, and of
course Valkyries seem to be inherently pretty easy to ascend).

Explanation: I suck at Nethack <g>. Even if it were actually me there
in the dungeon, my own living self risking my actual life, I *still*
would probably not proceed cautiously enough. <shrug>. And I DO treat
it as a game, and DO give in to 'oh well let's just try hitting this
creature one last time' or 'what the heck, I'll read the unknown
scroll' whims. I just find it really difficult to take low-level
characters seriously. So the survival rate for my characters up to,
say, explvl 10 is EXTREMELY low :-P

However, I get more attached to the higher level ones, and am more
careful on their behalf. The frequency-distribution of life span for
my characters is highly skewed -- anyone who is still alive at explvl
11 or so is quite likely to make it at *least* thru the Quest and
Castle and into the Valley of the Dead. The first time I ever got the
Amulet, I died on the plane of Air, and the second (and to date only
other) time I got the Amulet, I ascended. I *don't* think it's because
I was so overequipped or because the later game is necessarily so easy
- I think it's just that I play differently once I've invested more
into a character.

>From my extensive experience with killing off characters before the
Quest ;-), I don't think that, for me, luck is all that big an issue
(unless there is a spectacular lack of it in a particular game, such
as a complete failure of a Tourist to turn up decent armor or weapon
of any sort). If anything, wizards of mine who begin with really nifty
starting equipment -- +oIdentify or +oCharming, /oLightning, magic
marker -- seem to have a higher-than-average chance of dying an early
ignominious death :-P

For me, I think the thing that enabled me to finally ascend was mainly
that some basic principles *finally* got pounded well enough into my
head that I actually obey them: don't go around burdened, make supply
depots of useful items rather than trying to keep 'em all with you,
don't try to save everything for later if it's needed *now*, run away
run away run away!, don't ever get surrounded, keep a close eye on the
status line of the screen, and above all, do not DO NOT eat that tin
of spinach while satiated :-P  Well, I guess there was also a small
but signficant element of having, by now, internalized pretty good
strategies for dealing with situations that initially bamboozled me,
such as monsters that teleport themselves out in between hits.

However, the thing that keeps me from ascending regularly is basically
my unwillingness to pay enough attention to lower-level characters to
keep them alive long enough for me to get really interested in them
;-)

But note this: my husband and I sometimes play nethack together. That
is, he is the one Actually Playing Nethack, and I "just" sit there and
kibbitz (pretty extensively since he hasn't played all that much and
has even less patience or self-control than I do). (I don't know how
he puts up with this <g>). And you know what? On just our third
character played this way, we've got a knight searching for the
vibrating square. On our own, neither of us has ever had a knight
survive past about explvl 9 before. Yet I think this one has a good
shot at ascending.

So it seems that two heads are better than one, for us - it is MUCH
easier to recognize another person's recklessness than your own.
Apparently, one stupid player + another stupid player = one prudent
successful player. Go figure :-)


